WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:05.102
Figure four. First workflow.

00:07.800 --> 00:27.732
In chat, the agent decides what happens next, every turn. That's wrong for work you want done the same way every time. A workflow hands the control flow to a file instead: what runs, in what order, and when. The agent still works inside it, on a leash the file declares.

00:28.432 --> 00:33.692
Keelson ships starter workflows. The smoke test runs one of every kind of node.

00:35.432 --> 00:39.830
Nine nodes, all green, in a few seconds. Several started at once.

00:40.992 --> 01:02.268
Those nodes come in three families. Agent nodes run model turns: a prompt, a named command, a loop. Deterministic nodes, bash and scripts, run code with no model at all. Control nodes pause for a human, or end a branch on purpose. The mix sets how much is judgment, and how much is fact.

01:03.494 --> 01:07.810
The browser runs the same workflow, and shows the executor at work.

01:09.247 --> 01:15.483
Every node whose dependencies are met starts right away, and the rest start the moment theirs settle.

01:16.566 --> 01:20.370
And both runs are still here: the one from the terminal, and this one.

01:22.353 --> 01:41.693
Because a run is a record. Keelson stores a row for the run and one per node: its output, its timing, the model it really ran on, and a hash of the exact definition. A failed run resumes from the first unfinished node, without redoing finished work.

01:42.293 --> 01:45.497
The file decides. The run remembers.

01:48.197 --> 01:51.777
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.
